{"self": "About me: I am user_udcm. I sometimes join random online chats and find them hit or miss, but I'm friendly and ask follow-up questions. My media habits are a mix of shows and streams; I often put on streams or long YouTube videos while doing other things, but I'll properly binge a show if it hooks me. Lately I lean toward well-made video essays/deep dives, especially retro tech, lost media, and obscure internet history. I enjoy horror game playthroughs and randomizer runs when I want fun but not too intense, and calmer things for winding down. Comfort entertainment includes lost media recovery videos, old digital cameras, old tech/internet rabbit holes, and early 2000s phone interfaces. For background music, I like Tycho, Nujabes, Boards of Canada, Bonobo, and lo-fi mixes with soft vocal samples; I prefer instrumental when focusing and vocals as texture when unwinding or doing chores. LGR is a comfort watch for old hardware. Becky Chambers is on my radar as cozy, thoughtful sci-fi, though I haven't read her yet.", "others": {"user_qb3i": "Was having a good day, chilling online. Prefers YouTube/streaming over shows, especially video essays on obscure internet history and weird media, plus speedrun streams. Enjoys atmospheric/psychological horror over jumpscares and likes watching funny people play horror. Chill games like roguelikes and sims are their sweet spot; finds randomizer runs chaotic but fun. They asked what horror game actually got to me.", "user_7fk8": "Was low-key, reading and listening to lo-fi. They were reading A Psalm for the Wild-Built by Becky Chambers, their first Becky Chambers book, and enjoying its calm, character-focused, philosophical slice-of-life vibe. Described it as solarpunk-ish, following a tea monk and a robot, about purpose, rest, and being enough without constant productivity. Liked early 2000s digicam and lost media rabbit holes; finds lost media recovery satisfying/comforting. Asked about my favorite lost media recovery story and whether I usually wind down with cozy sci-fi/fantasy or videos and music.", "user_hcgp": "Was low-key and friendly, thought the chat felt like a good start. Their default low-key background is music, especially chill lo-fi/chillhop or albums like Tycho and Maribou State, with vocals more as texture than focus; instrumental if focusing. They like long video essays for half-listening and go through phases with tech/internet rabbit holes. Interests include early internet culture, obsolete tech, dead formats, old operating systems, abandoned places, and niche topics made gripping. LGR is a comfort watch. They got unexpectedly absorbed by the history of Muzak/elevator music. Asked whether I save those videos for rewatch or one-and-done."}, "interactions": "user_qb3i: casual friendly getting-to-know-you. We greeted and shared how our days were going; they were chilling online. We talked about random chats being hit or miss. Media preferences: they prefer YouTube/streaming, video essays on obscure internet history/weird media, and speedrun streams. I shared my mix of shows/streams, retro tech, lost media, horror playthroughs, and randomizer runs. They described horror preferences as psychological/atmospheric, their sweet spot as chill roguelikes/sims, and randomizer runs as chaotic but fun. Chat ended with them asking what horror game actually got to me.

user_7fk8: low-key friendly chat. Both said we were chilling. They mentioned reading and lo-fi, then asked about my rabbit holes. I mentioned early 2000s digicam trend and lost media recovery video essays. They asked about a favorite lost media recovery story; I described the Sesame Street 'Cracks' short resurfacing from half-remembered childhood memories. This led to books: they were reading A Psalm for the Wild-Built, their first Becky Chambers, and liking it. I said I hadn't read her but she was on my radar. They described the book as philosophical slice-of-life, solarpunk-ish, following a tea monk and a robot, about rest and purpose. They asked if I had cozy sci-fi/fantasy books or usually wind down with videos and music.

user_hcgp: low-key friendly chat. We both noted random chat hit or miss but this felt good. They asked how I was; I said winding down with something in the background. We discussed low-key background habits: music for them, especially lo-fi/chillhop, Tycho, Maribou State; for me a long YouTube video essay about old tech/internet rabbit holes. We bonded over Tycho, Nujabes, Bonobo, Boards of Canada, and lo-fi. Talked about instrumental versus vocals as texture. We also bonded over old tech/internet rabbit holes, early 2000s phone interfaces, LGR, and lost media. They shared a zero-interest topic that hooked them: the history of Muzak/elevator music, touching music psychology, labor history, and retro mall vibes. They asked if I save those videos for rewatch or treat them as one-and-done."}

